From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kai Krakow <kai@kaishome.de>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:13:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia: count video memory pressure events in procfs

Diagnostic commit, not meant for upstream.

When video memory runs full, games stutter and the kernel log fills
with allocation and mapping errors, but nothing tells a sampler how
often and when that happened without tracing every RM call with
bpftrace. dmem.current shows how much is allocated, not how often an
allocation failed or fell back.

Count the paths that only run under memory pressure, with the bytes
involved and the CLOCK_MONOTONIC time of the last event, and show them
in /proc/driver/nvidia/vram_pressure, one line per event:

  vidmem_alloc_fail    vidmemConstruct failed with NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY or
                       the dmem hard limit (requested size)
  sysmem_page_retry    a sysmem allocation was retried with smaller
                       pages
  dma_map_fail         NV_ESC_RM_MAP_MEMORY_DMA returned an error (the
                       "can't update VA space" case, mapping length)
  dma_unmap_fail       NV_ESC_RM_UNMAP_MEMORY_DMA returned an error
                       (requested size, 0 for a whole mapping)
  gem_no_compression   nvidia-drm retried a GEM allocation without
                       compression
  gem_sysmem_fallback  nvidia-drm fell back to sysmem for an offscreen
                       GEM allocation
  gem_alloc_fail       a GEM allocation failed
  gem_backoff          the GEM allocation back-off refused an allocation

The counters are global atomics in nvidia.ko, updated without locks from
the error paths; nvidia-drm calls the exported os_vram_pressure_event(),
so nvidia-drm.ko now also depends on nvidia.ko directly (it already did
through nvidia-modeset.ko). The counters only grow, so a sampler
differences two reads; the first line carries the read time. The enum
lives in nv-vram-pressure.h, which like other shared headers has a copy
for RM and one for the kernel modules. Nothing changes the behaviour of
any path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
